vector<int> previousSmaller(vector<int>& arr) {
    int n = arr.size();
    vector<int> ans(n, -1);
    stack<int> st;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        while (!st.empty() && st.top() >= arr[i]) {
            st.pop();
        }
        if (!st.empty()) {
            ans[i] = st.top();
        }
        st.push(arr[i]);
    }
    return ans;
};

1. Intuition
For every element, we need to find the nearest smaller element on its left.
A brute-force solution checks all previous elements, taking O(n²) time.
We can optimize this using a monotonic increasing stack.
Since we need information from the left, we traverse the array left to right.

2. Approach
Create an answer array initialized with -1.
Create an empty stack to store elements.
Traverse the array from left to right.
While the stack is not empty and the top element is greater than or equal to the current element, pop it because it cannot be the previous smaller element.
If the stack is not empty, the top of the stack is the previous smaller element.
Store it in the answer array.
Push the current element onto the stack.

3. Why This Works
The stack always contains elements in increasing order.
Before processing the current element, all elements that are greater than or equal to it are removed.
Therefore, the top of the stack is always the nearest smaller element on the left.

4. Algorithm
Initialize answer array with -1.
Create an empty stack.
Traverse from left to right.
While stack is not empty AND
      stack.top() >= current element:
      Pop
If stack is not empty:
      answer[i] = stack.top()
Push current element.
Return answer.

Complexity
Time Complexity : O(n)
Space Complexity: O(n)
Reason:
Every element is pushed exactly once.
Every element is popped at most once.
